package main

// dissent_plan.go — Wave 12 D8, second Dissent port: src/coordination.ts (the
// dissent.work.v1 work contract, the executor/assurance qualification
// registries, the coordination runtime authority, compileIntelligencePlan,
// executionProfileId, sealPlan / parseIntelligencePlan) and src/assurance.ts
// (work-class classifier + risk escalation). Pure and deterministic: no I/O,
// no wiring into any product path. Pinned byte-for-byte by
// testdata/dissent_plan_golden.json (generated by the real TypeScript over
// TEST-ONLY registries; regenerate or audit with testdata/dissent-plan-golden.mjs,
// which records the sha256 of every source it ran).
//
// Inputs are JSON-decoded values (map[string]any / []any / float64 / string /
// bool / nil) so the validation mirrors zod exactly: strict objects (unknown
// keys refuse), enums, ranges, nullable fields, and the contract's defaults
// (risk, humanReviewRequired, assurance, quality, metadata, consequences),
// which are applied BEFORE contractSha256 is taken — exactly as the TS parse
// output is what gets hashed. Registries and the tenant policy carry no
// defaults, so their hashes are taken over the validated input as-is.
//
// EVERY refusal carries a code (dissentPlanError.Code): contract_invalid,
// registry_invalid, runtime_invalid, plan_invalid, plan_identity_mismatch —
// including the ones raised past the parsers, where a canonicalization fails
// on bytes reader.str never checked. Callers switch on that code, so a bare
// error is a hole, not a smaller bug; the code names the INPUT that owns the
// value (the assurance registry is a runtime-authority field, so its digest
// failures are runtime_invalid). The TS throws zod errors without codes; the
// plan STATUSES
// (clarification_required / policy_blocked / qualification_required / ready)
// are the fail-closed gates and are reproduced verbatim. routing.ts's
// INSUFFICIENT_PROVIDER_COVERAGE belongs to the (not yet ported) provider
// router, not to this kernel; the constant is declared here so the code
// vocabulary is in one place.
//
// The exact-set work-class match (a route must be qualified for EXACTLY the
// detected classes) is kept as in TS; the STRIDE superset relaxation comes
// later behind an option.

import (
	"encoding/json"
	"fmt"
	"math"
	"regexp"
	"sort"
	"strings"
)

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// ECMAScript semantics the Go standard library does not share
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

// dissentMaxSafeInteger is Number.MAX_SAFE_INTEGER. zod's .int() accepts any
// Number.isInteger value (up to ~1.8e308), which Go's int cannot hold: the
// float64 -> int conversion of, say, 2^63 is implementation-defined and the
// assurance critical-path sum of three such seats WRAPS to a small positive
// number that slips under the deadline gate the TypeScript fails closed on.
// Every integer this kernel reads is therefore refused above the range where
// a JS number is still an exact integer — which is also the range where the
// TypeScript itself has already fail-closed (the contract deadline ceiling is
// 3_600_000 ms, so no larger timeout can ever be selected or fit a critical
// path), and it keeps the three-seat sum far below int64 overflow.
const dissentMaxSafeInteger = float64(1<<53 - 1)

// dissentJSWhitespace is JavaScript's \s as an RE2 character class. Go's \s
// is only [\t\n\f\r ]; ECMAScript's is WhiteSpace + LineTerminator, i.e. every
// Unicode space separator plus \v, NBSP, U+2028/9 and the BOM. A corpus pasted
// from Word, Google Docs or HTML routinely separates words with U+00A0, and
// with Go's \s the consequence detector silently misses it.
const dissentJSWhitespace = `[\t\n\v\f\r \x{00a0}\x{1680}\x{2000}-\x{200a}\x{2028}\x{2029}\x{202f}\x{205f}\x{3000}\x{feff}]`

// dissentStringLength is JavaScript's String.length — UTF-16 code units, the
// unit every zod .min()/.max() on a string measures. Counting runes instead
// lets an astral-character payload be accepted by one implementation and
// rejected by the other (150k emoji is 300k code units, over context's 200k
// cap; "😀😀" is 4 units, over objective's 3-unit floor).
//
// It shares dissentUTF16Length's walk with the canonicalizer's key
// comparator, so a lone surrogate counts as the ONE code unit JS sees. A
// `for _, char := range value` loop instead makes Go's decoder mangle each of
// the three WTF-8 bytes into a separate U+FFFD and count 3.
func dissentStringLength(value string) int {
	return dissentUTF16Length(value)
}

// dissentToLowerJS is String.prototype.toLowerCase. Go's strings.ToLower is
// the SIMPLE lowercase mapping; ECMAScript applies the FULL locale-insensitive
// mapping, which additionally expands U+0130 (LATIN CAPITAL LETTER I WITH DOT
// ABOVE) to "i" + U+0307 COMBINING DOT ABOVE. That combining mark breaks the
// ASCII word boundaries the classifier patterns rely on, so "PRODUCTİON"
// matches the production signal in Go and does not in JS.
//
// The only other locale-insensitive special case is Final_Sigma (Σ lowercases
// to ς, not σ, at the end of a word). It cannot change any outcome here:
// every classifier pattern is pure ASCII and RE2's \b, like a non-unicode
// JS \b, treats σ and ς alike as non-word characters.
func dissentToLowerJS(value string) string {
	if !strings.ContainsRune(value, '\u0130') {
		return strings.ToLower(value)
	}
	return strings.ToLower(strings.ReplaceAll(value, "\u0130", "\u0069\u0307"))
}

